#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Make a rutracker release of albums of the bucket: the .torrent, the topic title and the BBCode description.
In cmus: ":bind common S shell footclient -N <repo>/contrib/cmus-b2-release" opens it in a new window with the
bucket's folders as a tree (genre, artist, album), open at the playing album: Space marks albums or whole
folders, Enter goes on. Several albums make one release each, or one release together. t opens the marked
albums (or the one under the cursor) in Picard first, as K does in cmus (contrib/cmus-b2-tag).

The files are read from the bucket mount (bucket_dir). Every track is probed (codec, bitrate, sample rate,
decode errors, flac -t for FLAC) and two spectrograms are drawn. Claude fills in what the tags do not say
(genre, country, the artist's name in the original script, the forum section, "about" and line-up; it can
search the web) and changes the draft by what you write until you accept it: tags, names, deleting stray files
and adding files of ~/Downloads to the albums (a cover you found). The notes you leave as they are are not
shown again. Then the cover (at most 500 px)
and the spectrograms are uploaded (to imageban.ru with IMAGEBAN_KEY, else imgbox.com), the .torrent and
the texts are written to ~/Downloads/rutracker/<release>/, and the title and the description go to the
clipboard one after the other, while Firefox shows the rutracker search for the album, to check it is not there yet.
Nothing is posted: you create the topic on rutracker yourself. rutracker keeps the info hash, so once you
download its .torrent (with your passkey) to ~/Downloads, the window finds it and hands it to cmus-b2-seed,
which seeds it from the bucket (view 9).

Needs python-libtorrent, python-anthropic, python-mutagen, python-requests, ffmpeg, flac, wl-copy and
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (in the environment or as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... in ~/.config/cmus-b2/env).
"""
